'b1-f56f538f', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to
a gold signet ring from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the ancient site
of Thisbê in Boeotia, central Greece.[5] The ring formed part of a hoard of 
gold signet rings and bead‑seals recovered in 1915 from a tomb near the village
of Dombrena and has been associated in scholarly literature with the Homeric 
figure of Nestor because of its elaborate imagery and perceived royal 
character.[5] Since its first publication in the early twentieth century, the 
ring has attracted sustained attention for its engraved scene, which has been 
interpreted in relation to Minoan and Mycenaean religious imagery and to 
conceptions of the afterlife.[5] More recent scholarship has revisited the 
ring’s iconography in the context of debates about Aegean forgeries, drawing on
comparisons with other glyptic material from Crete and the Greek 
mainland.[2]\n\nThe ring is generally described as a high‑status signet, 
manufactured in gold with an elongated oval bezel that carries a complex 
figural composition.[5] Its find context in a Mycenaean chamber tomb together 
with other luxury items, including glass‑paste necklace elements and gold 
ornaments, suggests that it belonged to an individual of elevated social 
standing.[5] Typological comparison with Minoan and Mycenaean jewelry places 
the ring and its associated hoard in the later phases of the Late Bronze Age, 
roughly corresponding to the early Late Minoan IIIb period.[5] The ring has 
played a role in wider discussions of Mycenaean religion and glyptic art, and 
it continues to serve as a case study in the methodological use of iconography 
to evaluate the authenticity of Aegean artifacts.[2]\n', 'evidence_refs': 
