'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 
'b3-48824fa6', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is described in early publication
as part of a "truly royal treasure" of gold rings and bead‑seals from a 
Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê.[5] Within this assemblage, the 
ring is characterized as a gold signet, with a bezel of elongated oval form 
engraved in intaglio.[5] One ring in the same corpus is recorded as having a 
bezel measuring 28 × 17 mm, a size consistent with high‑status Aegean signet 
rings, though the excerpted measurement does not specify whether it refers to 
the Ring of Nestor itself or to a close parallel.[5][c11] Comparative data on 
ring sizes from medieval, Roman, and Greek material indicate average inner 
diameters between about 17 and 19 mm, providing a general framework for 
understanding the scale of such objects, although individual dimensions for the
Ring of Nestor have not been specified in the available sources.[5][c10]\n\nThe
engraved scene on the bezel has been discussed in relation to Late Minoan 
glyptic style and iconography. In the broader comparative corpus, Late Minoan 
intaglios frequently show figures with heads rendered in very summary fashion, 
while emphasizing elaborate garments, such as skirts with multiple flounces; 
one bead‑seal from the Cretan village of Mokhòs depicts a goddess with a 
nine‑flounced skirt and accompanying girls dressed in a similar manner.[5][c8] 
Other Minoan images, including a bronze female figure in the Berlin Museum with
a triple knot of serpents at the back of the head, provide comparanda for 
complex hairstyles and divine attributes in Aegean religious art.[5][c9] The 
Dombrena hoard also included numerous small objects of glass‑paste and faience,
some of which formed parts of necklaces, indicating a broader decorative 
repertoire into which the Ring of Nestor’s imagery has been integrated in 
stylistic discussions.[5][c5]\n\nModern iconographic studies have approached 
the Ring of Nestor as a key example of how detailed visual analysis can inform 
questions of authenticity and cultural attribution. Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 
article "Some Observations on the Iconography of the \'Ring of Nestor\'" 
discusses the ring’s motifs within long‑running debates about the genuineness 
of certain Aegean rings, including the so‑called Ring of Minos.[2][c1] 
According to summaries of this study, the composition on the Ring of Nestor has
been examined for features such as incurved altars and other architectural or 
cultic elements, which are compared with securely provenanced Minoan and 
Mycenaean material in order to assess whether the ring’s imagery could have 
been devised by a modern forger.[2][c2] The same summaries indicate that 
technical analyses of the ring have produced contradictory results and that 
Eliopoulos therefore gives particular weight to iconographic consistency and 
anomalies.[2][c2] A further suggestion reported from this work is that aspects 
of the ring’s iconography may echo cultural practices of the 1920s, a 
hypothesis presented as a possibility within the broader authenticity debate 
rather than as a definitive conclusion.[2][c3]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 
