"# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to a 
gold signet ring from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the ancient site 
of Thisbê in Boeotia, central Greece.[5] The ring formed part of a hoard of 
gold signet rings and bead‑seals recovered in 1915 from a tomb near the village
of Dombrena and has been associated in scholarly literature with the Homeric 
figure of Nestor because of its elaborate imagery and perceived royal 
character.[5] Since its first publication in the early twentieth century, the 
ring has attracted sustained attention for its engraved scene, which has been 
interpreted in relation to Minoan and Mycenaean religious imagery and to 
conceptions of the afterlife.[5] More recent scholarship has revisited the 
ring’s iconography in the context of debates about Aegean forgeries, drawing on
comparisons with other glyptic material from Crete and the Greek 
mainland.[2]\n\nThe ring is generally described as a high‑status signet, 
manufactured in gold with an elongated oval bezel that carries a complex 
figural composition.[5] Its find context in a Mycenaean chamber tomb together 
with other luxury items, including glass‑paste necklace elements and gold 
ornaments, suggests that it belonged to an individual of elevated social 
standing.[5] Typological comparison with Minoan and Mycenaean jewelry places 
the ring and its associated hoard in the later phases of the Late Bronze Age, 
roughly corresponding to the early Late Minoan IIIb period.[5] The ring has 
played a role in wider discussions of Mycenaean religion and glyptic art, and 
it continues to serve as a case study in the methodological use of iconography 
to evaluate the authenticity of Aegean artifacts.[2]\n\n## Discovery and 
Archaeological Context\n\n暂无可核实的补充资料。\n\n## Description and 
Iconography\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is described in early publication as part of 
a \"truly royal treasure\" of gold rings and bead‑seals from a Mycenaean 
rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê.[5] Within this assemblage, the ring is 
characterized as a gold signet, with a bezel of elongated oval form engraved in
intaglio.[5] One ring in the same corpus is recorded as having a bezel 
measuring 28 × 17 mm, a size consistent with high‑status Aegean signet rings, 
though the excerpted measurement does not specify whether it refers to the Ring
of Nestor itself or to a close parallel.[5][c11] Comparative data on ring sizes
from medieval, Roman, and Greek material indicate average inner diameters 
between about 17 and 19 mm, providing a general framework for understanding the
scale of such objects, although individual dimensions for the Ring of Nestor 
have not been specified in the available sources.[5][c10]\n\nThe engraved scene
on the bezel has been discussed in relation to Late Minoan glyptic style and 
iconography. In the broader comparative corpus, Late Minoan intaglios 
frequently show figures with heads rendered in very summary fashion, while 
emphasizing elaborate garments, such as skirts with multiple flounces; one 
bead‑seal from the Cretan village of Mokhòs depicts a goddess with a 
nine‑flounced skirt and accompanying girls dressed in a similar manner.[5][c8] 
Other Minoan images, including a bronze female figure in the Berlin Museum with
a triple knot of serpents at the back of the head, provide comparanda for 
complex hairstyles and divine attributes in Aegean religious art.[5][c9] The 
Dombrena hoard also included numerous small objects of glass‑paste and faience,
some of which formed parts of necklaces, indicating a broader decorative 
