'b1-f56f538f', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to
a gold signet ring from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the ancient site
of Thisbê in Boeotia, central Greece.[5] In the first detailed publication of 
the tomb group, Arthur Evans presented the ring as the most elaborate member of
a hoard of gold signet rings and bead‑seals recovered in 1915 from a chamber 
tomb near the village of Dombrena and compared its imagery with Homeric 
descriptions of Nestor’s cup to underscore its perceived royal 
character.[5][c4] Since Evans’s study, the ring has attracted attention for its
engraved scene, which has been discussed in relation to Minoan and Mycenaean 
religious imagery and to conceptions of the afterlife.[5] In 2013, Theodore 
Eliopoulos published a reassessment of the ring’s iconography in the journal 
Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in 
Oriente, situating it within wider debates on Aegean forgeries and the 
authenticity of high‑profile rings such as the Ring of 
Minos.[2][c1][c2]\n\nEvans described the ring as a high‑status signet 
manufactured in gold, with an elongated oval bezel bearing a complex figural 
composition in intaglio.[5] The ring’s association with a Mycenaean chamber 
tomb that also contained luxury items, including glass‑paste necklace elements 
and other gold ornaments, indicates that it belonged to an individual of 
elevated social standing within Late Bronze Age Boeotia.[5][c5] On the basis of
comparative work by Alan Wace, Evans dated the necklace components in the same 
tomb group to the period of the Dimini jewels, corresponding to early Late 
Minoan IIIb, and treated the ring as broadly contemporary with this 
horizon.[5][e21][e22][c5] Together with later studies of Mycenaean Pylos and 
the Palace of Nestor, in which the Late Bronze Age phases between about 1600 
and 1400 BC are examined as a formative stage of Mycenaean culture,[4][e7] the 
Ring of Nestor has become one of the objects used to explore connections 
between Mycenaean funerary practice, elite display, and the iconography of 
Aegean religion.[2][5]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 'e18', 'c4', 'e21', 'e22', 
'c5', 'c1', 'c2', 'e7'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 2, 
'bundle_token': 'b1-f56f538f', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor was found in a 
Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbê in Boeotia, by the 
modern village of Dombrena.[5] Arthur Evans reported that, according to his 
inquiries, the discovery was made in 1915 by a local peasant who excavated a 
chamber tomb cut into the rock, at a time when wartime conditions diverted 
finds away from official archaeological channels.[5][e17][e18][c4] Evans 
described the assemblage from this tomb as a treasure of gold signet rings and 
bead‑seals, together with associated ornaments and small objects in other 
materials.[5] The find was later exhibited to the Society for the Promotion of 
Hellenic Studies, providing one of the earliest public presentations of the 
ring and its accompanying hoard.[5]\n\nAdditional information about the tomb 
group and its surroundings derives from observations by W. A. Heurtley of the 
British School at Athens. Heurtley recorded three chamber tombs with dromoi in 
an olive grove on the Akropolis height of Thisbê, about a quarter of an hour 
northwest of Kakosi, and relayed the account of an older man who had fully 
excavated one tomb and claimed to have found a dagger and vases that were 
subsequently lost.[5][e19][c14] From a drawing made by this informant, Heurtley
identified one of the vases as a late‑type stirrup jar, supporting the 
attribution of the tombs to the late Mycenaean period.[5][e19][c14] Evans also 
mentioned other objects from the same group of tombs, including bronze 
spearheads, a short sword with a flanged hilt of very late Mycenaean type, an 
advanced‑form bronze razor, and a small perforated iron double‑axe head, though
he emphasized that these items were not authenticated as coming from the 
specific chamber that contained the gold treasure.[5][e20][c6]\n\nWithin the 
treasure, Evans singled out the Ring of Nestor as the most elaborate of the 
gold signets.[5] The hoard also included perforated glass‑paste elements with 
holes for attaching pendant gold disks, paste pendants in relief covered with 
gold foil, radiated glazed clay beads, and faience plaques with palm‑tree 
groups in relief.[5][e21][e22][c5] On the basis of Wace’s typological work, 
these necklace components were assigned to the period of the Dimini jewels, 
corresponding to early Late Minoan IIIb in Minoan chronological 
terms.[5][e21][e22][c5] Although Evans’s account relies partly on oral 
testimony and non‑systematic excavation, it remains the principal published 
source for the Ring of Nestor’s archaeological context and for the composition 
of the Dombrena hoard.[5]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 'e18', 'c4', 'e19', 
