{'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 'b3-48824fa6', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor
is described in Evans’s publication as part of a "truly royal treasure" of gold
rings and bead‑seals from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near 
Thisbê.[5][e17][c4] Within this assemblage, the ring is characterized as a gold
signet, with a bezel of elongated oval form engraved in intaglio.[5] One gold 
signet ring in the same corpus is recorded as having a bezel measuring 28 × 17 
mm, a size typical of substantial Aegean signet rings, although the excerpted 
measurement does not specify whether it refers to the Ring of Nestor itself or 
to a close parallel.[5][e24][c11] In one comparative sample of finger rings 
examined by Evans, average inner diameters were about 17 mm for medieval and 
Renaissance women’s rings from England, France, and Italy, 18 mm for English 
men’s rings, 19 mm for seven Roman specimens, and 18.5 mm for seven Greek 
specimens, providing contextual data for the scale of rings discussed in his 
study.[5][e25][c10]\n\nThe engraved scene on the Ring of Nestor’s bezel has 
been discussed in relation to Late Minoan glyptic style and iconography. In the
broader comparative corpus used by Evans, Late Minoan intaglios frequently show
figures whose heads are rendered in a very summary manner while their garments 
are elaborated in detail. A bead‑seal from the Cretan village of Mokhòs, 
previously published by Stephanos Xanthoudides, depicts a goddess with a skirt 
of nine flounces and accompanying girls in similar dress, and is cited as an 
example of this pattern.[5][e26][c8] Other Minoan images, such as a bronze 
female figure in the Berlin Museum with a triple knot of serpents at the back 
of the head, supply parallels for complex hairstyles and divine attributes in 
Aegean religious art.[5][e27][c9] Evans also drew attention to the widespread 
circulation of certain small Minoan objects, noting that one class of artifacts
related to the Dombrena hoard was so common that he owned about a dozen 
specimens from central and eastern Crete in his personal 
collection.[5][e28][c7][c13]\n\nModern iconographic studies have approached the
Ring of Nestor as a key example for evaluating authenticity and cultural 
attribution through imagery. Theodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 article "Some 
Observations on the Iconography of the \'Ring of Nestor\'" analyzes the ring’s 
motifs within long‑standing debates about the genuineness of Aegean rings, 
including the Ring of Minos.[2][c1] According to the available summaries of 
this work, Eliopoulos reviews generations of scholarship and highlights 
contradictions among technical analyses, arguing that iconographic 
comparison—particularly the depiction of incurved altars and other cultic or 
architectural elements—is crucial for assessing whether the ring’s composition 
is consistent with securely provenanced Minoan and Mycenaean 
material.[2][e2][e3][c2] The same summaries report that Eliopoulos entertains 
the possibility that certain aspects of the ring’s iconography might reflect 
cultural practices of the 1920s, using this hypothesis to frame questions about
potential modern influence on the imagery.[2][e3][c3] In Eliopoulos’s 
treatment, the Ring of Nestor functions both as an object of stylistic analysis
and as a test case for the methodological weight accorded to iconography in 
discussions of Aegean forgeries.[2]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 'c4', 'e24', 
