'bundle_token': 'b3-48824fa6', 'content': 'Evans situated the Ring of Nestor 
and the associated Dombrena hoard within the later phases of the Mycenaean 
period, relying on typological and stylistic comparison rather than 
stratigraphic excavation.[5] The presence of perforated glass‑paste necklace 
elements, paste pendants covered with gold foil, radiated glazed clay beads, 
and faience plaques with palm‑tree groups in relief led Evans, following Alan 
Wace, to assign these pieces to the date of the Dimini jewels, or early Late 
Minoan IIIb.[5][e21][e22][c5] On this basis, the hoard as a whole, including 
the Ring of Nestor, has generally been treated as belonging to a late stage of 
the Late Bronze Age, contemporary with advanced Mycenaean material culture in 
mainland Greece.[5][c5]\n\nThe cultural attribution of the Ring of Nestor’s 
imagery has been described in terms of a dialogue between Minoan and Mycenaean 
traditions. Evans emphasized parallels with Cretan glyptic art, including Late 
Minoan intaglios from Mokhòs and other sites, and with small Minoan objects 
that circulated widely and are represented in his personal 
collection.[5][e26][e28][c7][c8][c13] At the same time, the ring’s findspot in 
a Mycenaean chamber tomb in Boeotia and the association with weapons and 
utilitarian items of very late Mycenaean type, such as a bronze razor and a 
short sword with a flanged hilt, anchor the object within a mainland funerary 
context.[5][e20][c6] This combination of Cretan stylistic comparanda and 
Boeotian tomb context has contributed to discussions of how Minoan iconography 
was adopted, adapted, or reinterpreted in Mycenaean elite 
settings.\n\nFunctional interpretations of the Ring of Nestor are linked to its
form as a substantial signet and to its burial with other high‑value items. 
Evans’ description of the hoard as a "royal" treasure implies that the rings 
and bead‑seals served both practical and symbolic roles, marking status and 
possibly participating in ritual or funerary display.[5][e17][c4] Comparative 
measurements of ring sizes in Evans’s sample, with inner diameters around 18–19
mm for Greek and Roman men’s rings, suggest that the gold signets from Dombrena
were intended for adult wearers, although exact dimensions for the Ring of 
Nestor itself are not recorded.[5][e25][c10] Later work on Mycenaean Pylos and 
the Palace of Nestor, focusing on the period between approximately 1600 and 
1400 BC, has underscored the importance of rings and sealings in administrative
and religious practices, providing a broader framework in which high‑status 
rings like the Ring of Nestor can be understood as instruments of authority, 
identity, and participation in cult.[4][e7]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e21', 'e22',
'c5', 'e26', 'e28', 'c7', 'c8', 'c13', 'e20', 'c6', 'e17', 'c4', 'e25', 'c10', 
