{'chapters': [{'chapter_id': 1, 'bundle_token': 'b1-f56f538f', 'content': 'The 
Ring of Nestor is the conventional name given to a gold signet ring from a 
Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the ancient site of Thisbê in Boeotia, 
central Greece.[5] In the first detailed publication of the tomb group, Arthur 
Evans presented the ring as the most elaborate member of a hoard of gold signet
rings and bead‑seals recovered in 1915 from a chamber tomb near the village of 
Dombrena and compared its imagery with Homeric descriptions of Nestor’s cup to 
underscore its perceived royal character.[5][c4] Since Evans’s study, the ring 
has attracted attention for its engraved scene, which has been discussed in 
relation to Minoan and Mycenaean religious imagery and to conceptions of the 
afterlife.[5] In 2013, Theodore Eliopoulos published a reassessment of the 
ring’s iconography in the journal Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e
delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente, situating it within wider debates on Aegean
forgeries and the authenticity of high‑profile rings such as the Ring of 
Minos.[2][c1][c2]\n\nEvans described the ring as a high‑status signet 
manufactured in gold, with an elongated oval bezel bearing a complex figural 
composition in intaglio.[5] The ring’s association with a Mycenaean chamber 
tomb that also contained luxury items, including glass‑paste necklace elements 
and other gold ornaments, indicates that it belonged to an individual of 
elevated social standing within Late Bronze Age Boeotia.[5][c5] On the basis of
comparative work by Alan Wace, Evans dated the necklace components in the same 
tomb group to the period of the Dimini jewels, corresponding to early Late 
Minoan IIIb, and treated the ring as broadly contemporary with this 
horizon.[5][e21][e22][c5] Together with later studies of Mycenaean Pylos and 
the Palace of Nestor, in which the Late Bronze Age phases between about 1600 
and 1400 BC are examined as a formative stage of Mycenaean culture,[4][e7] the 
Ring of Nestor has become one of the objects used to explore connections 
between Mycenaean funerary practice, elite display, and the iconography of 
Aegean religion.[2][5]\n', 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 'e18', 'c4', 'e21', 'e22', 
'c5', 'c1', 'c2', 'e7'], 'disclosed_conflict_refs': []}, {'chapter_id': 2, 
