'chapter_id': 2, 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor was found in 1915 in a 
Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbê in Boeotia, by the 
modern village of Dombrena.[5] In his published account, Arthur Evans said that
the discovery was made by a local peasant who dug into the chamber tomb during 
wartime conditions, when the find initially passed outside official 
archaeological channels.[5][e17][e18][c4] Evans identified the find as a 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead‑seals and noted that it also included 
ornaments and other small objects of precious and colored materials.[5] The 
ring and its companions were later exhibited to the Society for the Promotion 
of Hellenic Studies.[5]\n\nW. A. Heurtley of the British School at Athens later
reported three chamber tombs with dromoi in an olive grove on the Akropolis 
height of Thisbê, about a quarter of an hour northwest of Kakosi.[5][e19][c14] 
He also recorded the testimony of an older man who had opened one of the tombs 
and found a dagger, vases, and sherds, most of which were lost; from the man’s 
drawing, Heurtley identified one vessel as a late stirrup jar.[5][e19][c14] 
Evans likewise mentioned objects from the wider tomb group, including bronze 
spearheads, a short sword with a flanged hilt of very late Mycenaean type, a 
bronze razor, and a small perforated iron double‑axe head, while cautioning 
that they were not securely authenticated from the chamber that held the gold 
treasure.[5][e20][c6]\n\nThe hoard associated with the ring also contained 
perforated glass‑paste elements for necklaces, paste pendants covered with gold
foil, radiated glazed clay beads, and faience plaques with palm‑tree 
reliefs.[5][e21][e22][c5] Evans treated these pieces as part of the same 
deposit and used them, together with Wace’s typological work, to place the 
associated ornament group in the period of the Dimini jewels, or early Late 
Minoan IIIb.[5][e21][e22][c5] Because the discovery was not made through a 
modern stratigraphic excavation, the archaeological context of the Ring of 
Nestor is known primarily through Evans’s publication and later observations at
the site.[5]', 'disclosed_conflict_refs': [], 'evidence_refs': ['e17', 'e18', 
'c4', 'e19', 'c14', 'e20', 'c6', 'e21', 'e22', 'c5']}, {'bundle_token': 
'b3-48824fa6', 'chapter_id': 3, 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is described in 
Evans’s publication as part of a "truly royal treasure" of gold rings and 
bead‑seals from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê.[5][e17][c4] 
Within this assemblage, the ring is characterized as a gold signet with an 
elongated oval bezel engraved in intaglio.[5] One gold signet ring in the same 
corpus is recorded as having a bezel measuring 28 × 17 mm; the excerpt does not
make clear whether this measurement refers to the Ring of Nestor itself or to a
comparison piece.[5][e24][c11] In one comparative sample of finger rings 
examined by Evans, average inner diameters were about 17 mm for medieval and 
Renaissance women’s rings from England, France, and Italy, 18 mm for English 
men’s rings, 19 mm for seven Roman specimens, and 18.5 mm for seven Greek 
specimens.[5][e25][c10]\n\nThe engraved scene on the bezel has been discussed 
in relation to Late Minoan glyptic style and iconography. In the comparative 
material Evans cites, Late Minoan intaglios often render heads in a very 
summary manner while elaborating dress and other details. A bead‑seal from 
Mokhòs, previously published by Stephanos Xanthoudides, depicts a goddess with 
a skirt of nine flounces and girls dressed in a similar way, and Evans uses it 
as a stylistic parallel.[5][e26][c8] Another parallel is a bronze female figure
in the Berlin Museum with a triple knot of serpents at the back of the head, 
which Evans cites as an example of serpent imagery in Minoan art.[5][e27][c9] 
He also notes that certain small Minoan objects are common enough to appear in 
multiple examples from central and eastern Crete, supporting his broader 
comparison between the Dombrena material and Cretan glyptic 
traditions.[5][e28][c7][c13]\n\nA later article by Theodore Eliopoulos, "Some 
Observations on the Iconography of the \'Ring of Nestor,\'" revisited the 
ring’s motifs in the context of authenticity debates about Aegean rings.[2][c1]
The article examines iconographic comparisons, including incurved altars and 
other cultic or architectural elements, and places the ring alongside the Ring 
of Minos in discussions of genuineness and modern forgeries.[2][e2][e3][c2] It 
also raises the possibility that some features of the iconography may reflect 
cultural practices of the 1920s.[2][e3][c3] In this literature, the Ring of 
Nestor serves as both a signet ring and a test case for iconographic analysis 
in Aegean archaeology.[2]', 'disclosed_conflict_refs': [], 'evidence_refs': 
['e17', 'c4', 'e24', 'c11', 'e25', 'c10', 'e26', 'c8', 'e27', 'c9', 'e28', 
'c7', 'c13', 'e2', 'e3', 'e4', 'c1', 'c2', 'c3']}, {'bundle_token': 
'b1-f56f538f', 'chapter_id': 4, 'content': 'Evans and Wace placed the 
associated Dombrena material in the later phases of the Mycenaean period on 
typological grounds.[5] The perforated glass‑paste necklace elements, paste 
pendants covered with gold foil, radiated glazed clay beads, and faience 
plaques with palm‑tree reliefs were assigned to the period of the Dimini 
jewels, or early Late Minoan IIIb.[5][e21][e22][c5] Because the ring was 
recovered in the same tomb group, it is generally treated as belonging to the 
same late Bronze Age horizon.[5][c5]\n\nThe ring’s cultural attribution is 
usually discussed through comparisons between mainland and Cretan art. Evans 
compared the Dombrena material with Late Minoan intaglios from Mokhòs and with 
other Minoan objects in his collection, presenting the ring as part of a 
broader Aegean visual tradition rather than as an isolated 
find.[5][e26][e28][c7][c8][c13] At the same time, the findspot in a Mycenaean 
chamber tomb in Boeotia and the association with weapons and other prestige 
objects tie the ring to a mainland funerary setting.[5][e20][c6] A 2013 article
by Theodore Eliopoulos also revisited the ring’s iconography in the context of 
authenticity debates about Aegean rings.[2][c1]\n\nAs a signet ring, the object
combined a wearable form with a seal face that could be used to impress a 
design into clay or wax, and its burial with gold ornaments suggests that it 
also functioned as a prestige item in mortuary display.[5] Evans described the 
hoard as royal, but the surviving evidence does not identify the original 
owner.[5][c4] Because the tomb was not excavated under modern stratigraphic 
control, the chronology of the ring remains relative and depends on comparison 
with associated material rather than direct dating.[5][c5]', 
