[AIMessage] Ring of Nestor

The Ring of Nestor is the modern scholarly name for a Mycenaean gold signet 
ring associated with a hoard of gold rings and bead-seals found in 1915 in a 
rock-cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, close to the village of 
Dombrena.[1] It is discussed in Aegean archaeology for its find context and for
later debates over its iconography and authenticity.[1][2]

== Discovery ==
In the account published in the Journal of Hellenic Studies, the find is 
described as a “truly royal treasure” of gold rings and bead-seals from a 
Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbê.[1] The discovery was made in 1915, and the 
report says wartime conditions kept it outside normal official channels; the 
hoard was reportedly found by a local peasant.[1] The same publication places 
the tomb group near Dombrena and notes related finds in the wider burial 
complex, including bronze spearheads, a late Mycenaean short sword, a bronze 
razor, and a small perforated iron double-axe head.[1]

== Description ==
The accessible published summary identifies the object as a gold signet ring, 
but the excerpt consulted does not provide a full catalogue description of the 
Ring of Nestor itself.[1] One note in the same discussion gives the bezel of a 
gold signet ring as 28 × 17 mm, although the excerpt does not make clear 
whether that measurement refers specifically to the Ring of Nestor.[1]

== Dating and interpretation ==
Because the ring comes from a tomb group rather than a securely stratified 
excavation, its date is usually discussed in relation to the broader Mycenaean 
context of the hoard.[1] In the same article, associated objects from the 
Dombrena tomb group are linked to the early Late Minoan IIIb period or to very 
late Mycenaean types, which places the ring within the closing phases of the 
Late Bronze Age Aegean.[1] A 2013 study by Theodore Eliopoulos revisited the 
ring’s iconography in the context of Aegean forgery and authenticity debates, 
and the available summary says the paper reexamined key motifs, compared them 
with other material, and considered whether some details may reflect 1920s 
cultural practices.[2]

== Later scholarship ==
The Ring of Nestor remains a reference point in discussions of Mycenaean and 
Minoan glyptic art, especially in work concerned with authenticity and 
iconographic interpretation.[2] The available summary of Eliopoulos’s article 
says that generations of scholars have debated the genuineness of the Ring of 
Nestor and the Ring of Minos, with iconographic analysis playing a central role
in that discussion.[2]

== References ==
